WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:05.240
You've seen how convolutions over 2D images works.

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:11.460
Now, let's see how you can implement convolutions over not just 2D images, but over 3-dimensional

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:12.460
volumes.

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:16.060
Let's start with an example.

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:22.760
Let's say you want to detect features not just in a grayscale image, but in a RGB image.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:30.300
So an RGB image might be, instead of a 6x6 image, it could be 6x6x3, where the 3 here

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:33.140
corresponds to the 3 color channels.

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:38.300
So you think of this as a stack of 3 6x6 images.

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:44.500
In order to detect edges or some other feature in this image, you convolve this not with a

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:55.060
3x3 filter as you had previously, but now with also a 3D filter that's going to be 3x3x3.

00:00:55.060 --> 00:01:05.320
So the filter itself will also have 3 layers corresponding to the red, green, and blue channels.

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:12.820
So to give these things some names, this first 6 here, that's the height of the image, that's

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:20.140
the width, and this 3 is the number of channels.

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:29.100
Your filter also similarly has a height, a width, and a number of channels.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:35.120
And the number of channels in your image must match the number of channels in your filter.

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:39.020
So these two numbers have to be equal.

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:43.420
We'll see on the next slide how this convolution operation actually works, but the output

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:46.780
of this will be a 4x4 image.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:49.420
And notice this is 4x4, you know, by 1.

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:54.510
There's no longer 3 at the end.

00:01:54.510 --> 00:02:02.110
Let's go through in detail how this works, but let's use a more nicely drawn image.

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:10.630
So here's the 6x6x3 image, and here's the 3x3x3 filter.

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:17.630
And this last number, the number of channels matches between the image and the filter.

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:25.870
So to simplify the drawing of this 3x3x3 filter, instead of drawing it as a stack of 3 matrices,

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:33.660
I'm also going to sometimes draw it as this 3-dimensional cube like that.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:39.400
So to compute the output of this convolution operation, what you would do is take the

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:45.580
3x3x3 filter and first place it in that upper leftmost position.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:54.260
So notice that this 3x3x3 filter has 27 numbers, 27 parameters as 3 cubed.

00:02:54.260 --> 00:03:04.500
And so what you do is take each of these 27 numbers and multiply them with the corresponding

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:07.580
numbers from the red, green, and blue channels of the image.

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:11.340
So take the first 9 numbers from the red channel, then the 3 beneath it from the

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:15.340
green channel, then the 3 beneath it from the blue channel, and multiply it with the

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:22.860
corresponding 27 numbers that are, I guess, covered by this yellow cube shown on the left.

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:29.380
Then add up all those numbers, and this gives you this first number in the output.

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:35.220
And then to compute the next output, you take this cube and slide it over by 1.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:42.980
And again, do the 27 multiplications, add up the 27 numbers, that gives you this next output,

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:48.660
do it for the next position over, that gives you the third output, and so on.

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:55.300
That gives you the fourth, and then one row down, and then the next one, the next one,

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:57.740
the next one, and so on.

00:03:57.740 --> 00:04:09.150
You get the idea until at the very end, that's the position you would have for that final output.

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:10.750
So what does this allow you to do?

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:12.710
Well, here's an example.

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:15.350
This filter is 3 by 3 by 3.

00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:22.710
So if you want to detect edges in the red channel of the image, then you could have

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:29.430
the first filter be 1, 1, 1, minus 1, minus 1, minus 1, as usual, and have the green channel

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:35.310
be all zeros, and have the blue filter be all zeros.

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:43.630
And if you have these three stacked together to form your 3 by 3 by 3 filter, then this

00:04:43.630 --> 00:04:50.110
would be a filter that detects edges, vertical edges, but only in the red channel.

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:56.470
Alternatively, if you don't care what color the vertical edges are in, then you might have

00:04:56.470 --> 00:05:03.550
a filter that's like this, where is this 1, 1, 1, minus 1, minus 1, minus 1 in all three

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:04.790
channels.

00:05:04.790 --> 00:05:10.870
So by setting the second alternative set of parameters, you then have an edge detector,

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:16.750
a 3 by 3 by 3 edge detector, that detects edges in any color.

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:21.510
And with different choices of these parameters, you can get different feature detectors out

00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:25.070
of this 3 by 3 by 3 filter.

00:05:25.070 --> 00:05:30.510
And by convention, in computer vision, when you have an input with a certain height, a

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:35.910
certain width, and a certain number of channels, then your filter will have a potentially different

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:39.710
height, different width, but the same number of channels.

00:05:39.710 --> 00:05:44.990
And in theory, it's possible to have a filter that maybe only looks at the red channel,

00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:51.590
or maybe a filter that looks at only the green channel and the blue channel.

00:05:51.590 --> 00:05:59.350
And once again, you notice that convolving a volume, a 6 by 6 by 3, convolved with a 3

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:08.010
by 3 by 3, that gives a 4 by 4, a 2D output.

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:15.170
Now that you know how to convolve on volumes, there's one last idea that will be crucial

00:06:15.170 --> 00:06:19.530
for building convolutional neural networks, which is what if you don't just want to detect

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:23.890
vertical edges, what if you want to detect vertical edges and horizontal edges, and maybe

00:06:23.890 --> 00:06:27.450
45 degree edges, and maybe 70 degree edges as well.

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:32.370
But in other words, what if you want to use multiple filters at the same time.

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:35.750
So here's the picture we had from the previous slide.

00:06:35.750 --> 00:06:40.050
We had 6 by 6 by 3 convolved with 3 by 3 by 3, this 4 by 4.

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:44.610
And maybe this is a vertical edge detector, or maybe it's learned to detect some other

00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:45.770
feature.

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:57.680
Now, maybe a second filter may be denoted by this orangeish color, which could be a horizontal

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:01.060
edge detector.

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:08.300
So maybe convolving it with the first filter gives you this first 4 by 4 output, and convolving

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:14.380
with the second filter gives you a different 4 by 4 output.

00:07:14.380 --> 00:07:19.020
And what you can do is then take these two 4 by 4 outputs, take this first one, put it

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:25.460
in front, and you can take this second filter output, and we draw it here, put it in the

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:27.780
back as follows.

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:35.660
So that by stacking these two together, you end up with a 4 by 4 by 2 output volume.

00:07:35.660 --> 00:07:41.060
And you can think of the volume as, you can draw this as a box, I guess, maybe look

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:42.060
like this.

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:48.580
So this would be a 4 by 4 by 2 output volume, which is the result of taking your 6 by 6

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:55.540
by 3 image and convolving it, or applying two different 3 by 3 filters to it, resulting

00:07:55.540 --> 00:08:02.340
in two 4 by 4 outputs that then get stacked up to form a 4 by 4 by 2 volume.

00:08:02.340 --> 00:08:09.560
And the two here comes from the fact that we use two different filters.

00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:13.840
So let's just summarize the dimensions.

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:22.080
If you have a n by n by number of channels input image, so in the example, this was 6 by 6

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:29.240
by 3, where n subscript capital C is the number of channels, and you convolve that with a

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:40.280
f by f by, and again, this should be the same nC, so this was 3 by 3 by 3.

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:46.720
And by convention, this and this have to be the same number.

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:57.200
Then what you get is a n minus f plus 1 by n minus f plus 1 by, and I'm going to use

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:03.840
this nC prime, or really, it's really nC of the next layer, but this is the number

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:06.960
of filters that you use.

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:12.920
So this, in our example, would be 4 by 4 by 2.

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:17.800
And I wrote these assuming that you use a stride of 1 and no padding, but if you use

00:09:17.800 --> 00:09:22.080
a different stride of padding, then this n minus f plus 1 would be affected in the

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:26.380
usual way, as we saw in the previous videos.

00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:31.840
So this idea of convolution on volumes turns out to be really powerful.

00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:36.080
Only a small part of it is that you can now operate directly on RGB images with

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:42.760
three channels, but even more important is that you can now detect two features,

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:48.280
like vertical and horizontal edges, or 10, or maybe 128, or maybe several hundred

00:09:48.280 --> 00:09:53.840
different features, and the output will then have a number of channels

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:59.130
equal to the number of features you are detecting.

00:09:59.130 --> 00:10:05.210
Oh, and as a note of notation, I've been using your number of channels to denote this

00:10:05.210 --> 00:10:07.130
last dimension in the literature.

00:10:07.130 --> 00:10:13.490
People will also often call this the depth of this 3D volume.

00:10:13.490 --> 00:10:17.850
And both notations, channels or depth, are commonly used in the literature, but I find

00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:23.010
depth more confusing because we usually talk about the depth of a neural network as well.

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:28.610
So I'm going to use the term channels in these videos to refer to the size of this

00:10:28.610 --> 00:10:32.330
third dimension of these filters.

00:10:32.330 --> 00:10:38.490
So now that you know how to implement convolutions over volumes, you now are ready to implement

00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:41.810
one layer of a convolutional neural network.

00:10:41.810 --> 00:10:43.610
Let's see how to do that in the next video.
